1:# notify-digest
2:
3:A small cron job that pulls a handful of feeds, filters the items per channel,
4:and sends each channel a digest.
5:
6:## Running
7:
8:```sh
9:cp config.example.toml config.toml
10:python3 digest.py --config config.toml --db digest.sqlite3
11:```
12:
13:`--dry-run` prints what would be sent instead of sending it.
14:
15:Requires Python 3.11+ (uses `tomllib`). No third-party dependencies.
16:
17:## How it works
18:
19:Each run fetches the feeds, asks the SQLite archive (`store.py`) which items
20:are new, and only dispatches the new ones to the channels.
21:
22:An item counts as already-seen when the archive holds a row with the same
23:source and the same feed id — the feed's `raw_id` when it has one, else the
24:permalink (blogroll posts have no raw id). Unchanged feeds therefore produce
25:no digests on later cron ticks; only items with a new feed id get through.
26:
27:`--dry-run` previews without touching the archive.
28:
29:## Layout
30:
31:| File | Purpose |
32:| --- | --- |
33:| `digest.py` | CLI entry point, the per-run loop |
34:| `feeds.py` | Fetching and normalising the three feed formats |
35:| `store.py` | SQLite archive of everything seen |
36:| `render.py` | Digest text |
37:| `channels.py` | Delivery (stdout, webhook) |
38:
39:The `fixtures/` directory holds two snapshots of the feeds taken at different
40:times, so you can work on this without network access. Point the config at
41:`snapshot-a` or `snapshot-b`.
42:
43:## Deployment
44:
45:Runs from cron on the utility box:
46:
47:```
48:*/15 * * * * cd /srv/notify-digest && python3 digest.py >> /var/log/digest.log 2>&1
49:```
